## What changed vs philo

| Aspect | philo | philo_2.0 |
|---|---|---|
| Fork locking | `if (left > right)` ordered lock | `lock_two_forks()` smart & polite |
| `ph_think` | Calculated stagger sleep for odd N | Just prints "is thinking" |
| `ph_routine` | Commented-out stagger delay code | Clean loop |
| Mutex names | `dead_lock` | `done_lock` (more accurate) |
| `ph_destroy_mutexes` | Stage-based | `ph_cleanup` (same logic, clearer name) |

The entire lock ordering complexity is replaced by the 10-line `lock_two_forks` function.

User: i just realized, i am not allowed pthread_mutex_trylock in this project. is there a way to implement this without?

GitHub Copilot: Correct — `pthread_mutex_trylock` is not in the allowed list. The smart & polite algorithm's entire trick is the non-blocking `try_lock`; without it, you can't back off after locking one fork, so the pattern can't be directly implemented.

## What you can do instead

With only blocking `pthread_mutex_lock`, the classical deadlock-free approach is **ordered locking**: always acquire forks in a consistent global order. Your old actions.c already had exactly this:

```c
first = p->left;
second = p->right;
if (first > second)        // order by memory address
{
    first = p->right;
    second = p->left;
}
pthread_mutex_lock(first);
pthread_mutex_lock(second);
```

This is Dijkstra's original solution. It's deadlock-free because circular wait is impossible — the fork with the lowest address is always acquired first, so no cycle can form.

Alternatively, you can use **philosopher parity** instead of addresses:

```c
// Even philos: left → right
// Odd  philos: right → left  (breaks the circle)
if (p->id % 2 == 0)
{
    pthread_mutex_lock(p->left);
    pthread_mutex_lock(p->right);
}
else
{
    pthread_mutex_lock(p->right);
    pthread_mutex_lock(p->left);
}
```

Both achieve the same thing. Parity is slightly cleaner to read.

## Does performance suffer?

For the 42 project: **no, it doesn't matter**. Hinnant's analysis is about throughput under zero-think-time benchmarks with 32 philosophers on 8 cores. Your evaluators are testing for:

1. No deadlock
2. No data races (helgrind clean)
3. Death detected within 10 ms
4. No starvation (within the tested parameters)

For 2–5 philosophers with realistic `time_to_die`/`time_to_eat` values, ordered locking is perfectly fair.

## The main practical fix for starvation

Ordered locking alone can cause **initial thundering herd**: all threads start simultaneously and contend hard. A standard fix for 42 is a **staggered start**: odd-numbered philosophers sleep for one `time_to_eat` before their first fork-grab. This lets even-numbered philosophers eat first, so the table reaches a stable alternating pattern quickly:

```c
// In ph_routine, before the loop:
if (p->id % 2 == 0)
    usleep(p->dinner->time_to_eat);  // let odd philos go first
```

Or the reverse — either way picks one side to go first.

## Conclusion for philo_2.0

Replace the `lock_two_forks()` function in actions.c with ordered locking (address-based like you had before, or parity-based). The rest of the clean structure from the previous design is fine to keep. The inability to use `trylock` removes the possibility of the smart & polite algorithm, but ordered locking + staggered start is a well-proven, evaluator-approved approach for this project.

